Overlay patch 0044 (VR R3): MAKE AUDIO SILENCE IMPOSSIBLE TO MISS, AND
RECOVERABLE.

THE REGRESSION. Build 1.0.1.3 had no audio at all on the Vision Pro
("sound no longer works. couldn't hear any audio." -- the user, 2026-09-03);
1.0.1.2 had audio. A full audit of 3a540e8..231f09a found that NOTHING in that
diff touches the audio path, the audio session, or any thread audio uses:
`Graph_ProcessGfxCommands` is called unconditionally from RunFrame after
Graph_Update returns, `func_800F3054()` (the whole audio update) sits OUTSIDE
graph.c's `if (!problem)` block, G_RDPFULLSYNC is a stubbed command handler, the
[2]->[3] eye arrays agree at all six declaration sites, and the CVar stash names
no gAudio* key. So the silence arrives through a RESOURCE or SESSION side
effect, and SoH has exactly one funnel where that produces total silence with no
crash and no log line:

    OTRGlobals.cpp, the producer guard
      if (AudioPlayer_Buffered() + SAMPLES_LOW * AUDIO_FRAMES_PER_UPDATE >
          AudioPlayer_GetDesiredBuffered()) { audio.processing = false; }

The audio thread self-pumps every 5 ms and is NOT gated on rendering, so a slow
or hung game thread cannot silence it. It can only go quiet if
AudioPlayer_Buffered() -- SDL_GetQueuedAudioSize -- stops FALLING, i.e. the
CoreAudio device has stopped draining the queue. On visionOS entering a full
ImmersiveSpace re-anchors the audio session (SohVisionApp.swift's
setIntendedSpatialExperience, re-applied on a 3 s timer) and can interrupt the
device; SDL neither notices nor resumes. The queue then sits permanently at
capacity, the guard skips forever, and the game is silent with no diagnostic.

Compounding it: SDLAudioPlayer::DoPlay THROWS AWAY SDL_QueueAudio's return
value, so if the queue call fails (allocation failure under the +375 MB
framebuffer step that 1.0.1.3's 4096 eye budget, HUD framebuffers and 8-slot
vertex pool introduced) it fails completely invisibly.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES. Three things, all __IOS__-only -- the macOS build and
every desktop build compile byte-for-byte vanilla.

(a) DoPlay checks SDL_QueueAudio's return and counts failures. A failing queue
    can never again be silent in both senses of the word.
(b) A STALL WATCHDOG in the audio thread. If the backend queue has been at or
    above the desired level for more than one second -- which, with a healthy
    device draining at 32 kHz, cannot happen -- the device is not draining. The
    watchdog clears the stale queue and re-issues SDL_PauseAudioDevice(dev, 0),
    which is exactly what recovers a device an interruption left paused. It
    counts its own recoveries so `vr audio` can say whether it ever fired.
(c) COUNTERS, published as plain globals the shell's `vr audio` reads:
    beats (the thread is alive at all), produced, skipped-by-the-guard, queue
    failures, watchdog recoveries, and the buffered/desired pair. the user's next
    session proves audio in one line instead of listening for it.

The counters live in the shell (app/ios/SohIosShell.m) so the iPhone build links
them too; nothing outside __IOS__ references them.

--- rev2 (VR R14): THE SILENT LAUNCH, WHICH THIS WATCHDOG COULD NOT SEE -------

the user, on 1.0.1.17: "Sometimes I launch and there's no audio. I have to force
quit and then there's audio. Not often, every once in a while."

The stall watchdog above fires when the queue STOPS FALLING -- samples_left +
SAMPLES_LOW * AUDIO_FRAMES_PER_UPDATE > desired, which with 528, R_UPDATE_RATE 3
and DesiredBuffered 2480 means more than ~896 frames sitting in the device for a
whole second. That is the right test for a device that opened and then stopped
draining. It is exactly the WRONG test for a device that never opened at all: if
SDL_OpenAudioDevice failed, mDevice is 0, SDL_GetQueuedAudioSize(0) returns 0
forever, samples_left is pinned at ZERO, the stall counter resets every tick,
produce_and_play runs at full rate and every SDL_QueueAudio call fails into a
device id of 0. Silent, and invisible to a watchdog that only knows how to
recognise a FULL queue. A relaunch cures it because the next AVAudioSession
activation usually succeeds -- which is the user's sentence, in full.

AND A ONE-SHOT FAILURE COULD LATCH FOREVER. Audio::InitAudioPlayer answers a
failed Init() with SetCurrentAudioBackend(AudioBackend::NUL), which writes
"null" into Window.AudioBackend and SAVES the config. One unlucky launch would
make an install permanently silent, with no UI anywhere in the app to undo it.
On iOS that latch is refused now.

rev2 adds four things:

(d) THE SESSION IS PREPARED BEFORE THE DEVICE IS OPENED, AND THE OPEN RETRIES.
    SDL's coreaudio backend activates AVAudioSession itself and gives up on the
    first refusal. SohIos_AudioPrepareSession (app/ios/SohIosShell.m) sets the
    playback category and activates the session, counting and logging failures;
    DoInit calls it before each of five attempts spread over ~1 s.
(e) THE DEVICE CAN BE REOPENED. SohIos_AudioReopenDevice does DoClose + Init,
    which is what recovers a device whose audio unit never started -- something
    SDL_PauseAudioDevice(dev, 0) cannot do, because there is nothing to unpause.
    Serialised by its own mutex, and harmless when audio was in fact fine: it
    reopens a working device to a working device.
(f) THE AUDIO THREAD SERVICES THE REQUEST. gSohAudioReopenReq is set by the
    shell's launch watchdog and consumed at the top of this loop, for the same
    one-writer reason the stall recovery lives here: closing a device underneath
    a live SDL_QueueAudio is a race. The watchdog calls the reopen itself only
    when the thread has stopped beating -- in which case nothing else is inside
    SDL and that call is the only one there is.
(g) AN EXCEPTION NO LONGER KILLS THE PROCESS. An uncaught throw under
    OTRAudio_Thread reaches std::terminate and aborts -- the `OTRAudio_Thread`
    cxx-terminate this project has carried as an open item since R10. The
    producer block is wrapped, the fault is COUNTED, and the thread keeps
    running.

The device id and the live backend are published too, so `vr audio` can say
"the device is 0" rather than leaving it to be inferred from a byte counter.

--- rev3 (VR R17 part B): THE ERROR LINE THAT DESTROYED THE EVIDENCE ----------

rev2's (a) made a failing queue impossible to miss. On 1.0.1.21 it made it
impossible to see anything else. The shell's launch watchdog fired a false
positive on every launch and reopened a healthy device; the reopened unit did not
play; every subsequent SDL_QueueAudio failed; and this SPDLOG_ERROR wrote 19,700
records in 80 milliseconds. The device's soh1.log came back as 10 MB carrying
nothing but that line, with spdlog's 10-file rotation having discarded the
session's entire history behind it -- so the round that needed the engine log had
none.

rev3 rate-limits it: the first 20 failures, then every thousandth, each carrying
the running total. Nothing else in the patch changes. gSohAudioQueueFails is
still incremented on every failure, so `vr audio` and the watchdog's two-window
rule (which reads it as proof the audio thread is alive) see every one of them --
only the log line is thinned, and it is thinned on the counter rather than on a
clock so the thinning is deterministic and testable on the host.

